package concept

medicinal_chemistry: {
  content: #"""
    *Medicinal chemistry* applies chemical principles to the discovery, design,
    synthesis, and evaluation of compounds intended to modify biological
    processes. It connects molecular structure to target recognition,
    physicochemical properties, pharmacokinetics, efficacy, and safety.

    A structure–activity relationship (SAR) compares related molecules to test
    how a controlled structural change alters a measured biological effect.
    Medicinal-chemistry decisions therefore balance target binding with factors
    such as ionisation, solubility, membrane permeability, metabolic stability,
    and off-target activity. A structural feature that improves one property
    can impair another, so optimization is iterative and depends on experimental
    evidence.

    This handbook uses drug case studies to connect molecular features with
    mechanism of action and observed pharmacology. The amantadine example
    illustrates how a rigid adamantane cage and a basic amine form a compact
    antiviral scaffold, while viral target mutations can erase its activity.
  """#
  related: ["specific/amantadine"]
}
